WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:24.061
Count Victor Lustig may have been the most
successful con man of the XXth Century. From

00:00:24.061 --> 00:00:28.170
the Belle Epoque to the Great Depression,
through the Roaring Twenties and across two

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:33.650
continents, this elegant swindler eluded the
grasp of the law while racking in hundreds

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:37.470
of thousands of Dollars.
Newspapers described him as ‘a story book

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:41.540
character’. Federal Agents said of him that
he was “as elusive as a puff of cigarette

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:44.830
smoke and as charming as a young girl’s
dream”

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:49.470
Today we remember him as Victor Lustig, the
man who sold the Eiffel Tower

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:57.960
A Bohemian Rhapsody
Count Victor Lustig was born on the 4th of

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:03.260
January 1890, into an aristocratic Austro-Hungarian
family. His birth place was the town of Hostinné,

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.509
back then part of the Province of Bohemia,
modern day Czech Republic.

00:01:07.509 --> 00:01:11.260
Or at least, that’s one of the stories he
told.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:15.610
Victor Lustig was born on the 4th of January
1890, the son of Ludwig, the middle-class

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:20.830
burgomaster (or mayor) of Hostinné.
Or maybe not.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:24.470
The man who would be later known as Count
Victor Lustig was born Robert V. Miller in

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:29.110
Hostinné, in a family he described as the
“poorest peasant people”, and was raised

00:01:29.110 --> 00:01:31.770
in a house made from stone, little more than
a cave.

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:34.750
Or was he?
The man who would be known as Victor Lustig,

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:40.950
Robert Miller and more than 40 other aliases
was born sometime in 1890, somewhere near

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:46.330
the border between Bohemia and Poland, from
two parents, whom we can safely guess were

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:50.439
a lady and a guy. Any more detail than that,
I would not bet my hard-earned money on it,

00:01:50.439 --> 00:01:52.080
provided YouTube actually allows me to make
some[TA2] . Relying on any of these stories

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:57.259
would imply trusting the word of he who would
grow up to become the King of Swindlers, the

00:01:57.259 --> 00:02:02.329
Arch Duke of Grifters, the CEO of Confidence
Tricks, Inc.

00:02:02.329 --> 00:02:06.470
On one detail we can all agree on: he was
a Bohemian, in both meanings of this word.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:11.890
He definitely came from the land of Bohemia
and he led a lifestyle that could be described

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:16.739
as ‘Bohemian’, at least according to my
handy Oxford English Dictionary:

00:02:16.739 --> 00:02:21.450
“A socially unconventional person, especially
one who is involved in the arts”

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:25.720
And what was the capital of the Bohemian Lifestyle,
or la vie bohemienne, if not Paris at the

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:31.329
height of the Belle Epoque? And that’s exactly
where we are going to pick up again the story

00:02:31.329 --> 00:02:34.829
of our protagonist, whom I am going to call
‘Victor’ from now on.

00:02:34.829 --> 00:02:38.880
Very little is known of Victor’s childhood
and early teens. But we know with a certain

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:43.160
degree of confidence that he lived in Paris
around 1908 and 1909. He may have lived there

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:48.130
to attend University, which backs the version
that he came from a middle-class background.

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:53.130
But apparently he spent little time studying,
being more suited to a life of gambling, womanising

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:58.329
and generally having great fun. Not everybody
agreed with his idea of fun, especially when

00:02:58.329 --> 00:03:02.520
it involved sleeping with somebody else’s
girlfriend. When Victor was 19 he was scarred

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:06.720
on his left cheek by the jealous lover of
his fille, a scar that would remain visible

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:10.570
for the rest of his life.
Victor’s bohemian lifestyle left other durable

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:15.770
marks on him, albeit more metaphorical. During
his formative years Victor became proficient

00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:19.500
in several languages, including English, French
and Italian.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:24.299
More importantly he started perfecting his
money-making skills – on the wrong side

00:03:24.299 --> 00:03:28.760
of the law, of course. Starting at entry level
as a pick-pocket, Victor soon graduated to

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:34.690
burglar and then branched out to street hustling
and finally earned a Masters in scamming and

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:39.709
confidence tricks. Especially card tricks:
according to a true crime magazine of his

00:03:39.709 --> 00:03:44.489
time, by his late teens
“Lustig could make a deck of cards do everything

00:03:44.489 --> 00:03:52.579
but talk.”
Across the Pond

00:03:52.579 --> 00:03:57.350
After clearing the pockets of many a Parisian,
Victor moved to a new level of conmanship:

00:03:57.350 --> 00:04:02.660
being a conman aboard cruise ships. Between
1909 and the outbreak of WWI Victor travelled

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:07.680
back and forth across the Atlantic, from France
to New York and back, picking his marks (in

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.400
other words: his victims) among the nouveaux
riches travelling in 1st Class.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:16.540
It was in this period that he created his
most famous persona, the Count Victor Lustig,

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:21.240
a nobleman from the fallen kingdom of Bohemia.
He acquired a set of well-tailored clothes

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:26.930
and affected his mannerisms to appear wealthy,
aristocratic – but never boastful, nor arrogant

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:32.410
– and most of all: worth of confidence.
Despite being in his early twenties he managed

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:37.069
to convince his marks that he was a Broadway
producer, in search of investors to put on

00:04:37.069 --> 00:04:41.409
a new dazzling musical show.
This evolution into a smoother style of swindling

00:04:41.409 --> 00:04:46.419
did not happen by magic. Victor needed a master
and he found one during one of these Atlantic

00:04:46.419 --> 00:04:51.650
trips: Nicky Arnstein, an American professional
gambler and con artist, famous for being the

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:56.350
husband of actress Fanny Brice. Nicky and
Fanny are now remembered as the main characters

00:04:56.350 --> 00:05:00.020
of the films “Funny Girl” and “Funny
Lady” with Barbra Streisand.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:04.370
Lustig would acknowledge Arnstein as the one
who taught him the true art of the con, the

00:05:04.370 --> 00:05:10.240
one who raised him from the status of promising
rookie to Smooth Scam-master General. Arnstein’s

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:14.689
teachings would influence the ‘10 Golden
Rules of the Con’ which Victor formalised

00:05:14.689 --> 00:05:21.050
later in his life. Curious about these ‘Golden
Rules’? I will give you two for the moment:

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:28.639
1. Be a patient listener. It is this, not
fast talking, that gets a con-man his coups.

00:05:28.639 --> 00:05:32.500
2. Never look bored.
In this spirit, I will ask you to continue

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:38.710
to listen patiently and try not to look too
bored. Let’s continue with Lustig’s life

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:45.699
for the moment …
Adventures in America

00:05:45.699 --> 00:05:50.759
When World War I broke out Transatlantic travel
for the rich became less frequent and more

00:05:50.759 --> 00:05:56.129
dangerous. In a way the U-Boots were responsible
for the next stage in Lustig’s career: the

00:05:56.129 --> 00:06:00.000
con artist settled in the US, mainly Kansas
City.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.020
As ever, the details of his life in this period
are sketchy and unreliable, but we know of

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:09.360
some juicy bits. During and immediately after
the War, Victor partnered with a sidekick

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:13.300
known as “Dapper” Dan Collins, whom the
New York Times described as

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:18.770
“a former circus lion tamer and death-defying
bicycle rider”.

00:06:18.770 --> 00:06:24.249
We love him already! Smooth Victor and Dapper
Dan’s main scam was called the “Rumanian

00:06:24.249 --> 00:06:27.040
money box.”
This was an ordinary box, fashioned to look

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:32.190
like a complicated machine, with rollers and
dials. If you put a banknote inside it, say

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:36.180
a 100 Dollar bill, and then inserted some
chemicals, the machine would take 6 hours

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:41.650
to print an identical copy of your money – so
realistic that no bank teller could identify

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:46.689
it as counterfeit. After 12 hours of recovery
time, the Rumanian box was ready to print

00:06:46.689 --> 00:06:50.669
another bill.
The trick behind it was elaborate but brilliant:

00:06:50.669 --> 00:06:54.680
Step one: collect a handful of 100 Dollar
bills from your bank, ensuring their serial

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:58.129
numbers are all sequential
Step two: alter the banknotes so that they

00:06:58.129 --> 00:07:02.250
all have the same serial number
Number three: keep one note for demonstration

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:05.259
purposes and then stuff the other notes into
the machine

00:07:05.259 --> 00:07:08.800
Then, find your marks and get them interested
in the machine

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:13.270
Now, demonstrate how the Rumanian box works.
Spend the six of hours of waiting time with

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:15.150
your marks, while you dine, wine and charm
them

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:20.770
Step six: open the box and – voila! There
are two banknotes now! How cool is that, your

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:23.879
money has doubled.
Next step: to further convince the marks,

00:07:23.879 --> 00:07:28.180
take them to a bank and deposit the freshly
printed bill. Watch them gasp and amaze when

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:31.110
the bank teller fails to realise it’s fake
money!

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:35.430
Gran Finale: sell the Rumanian box to your
mark for anything up to 10,000 Dollars. It

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:39.029
will repay for itself after all!
While the marks waited for the 12 hours for

00:07:39.029 --> 00:07:43.310
the machine to ‘rest’ before they used
it again, Lustig and Collins would skip town,

00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:47.680
ready to trick somebody else. The genius idea
for the operation was that nobody would report

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:51.490
to scam, as it would amount to a confession
of counterfeiting.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:55.659
A similar scam, in the sense that it exploited
the victim’s dishonest leanings, took place

00:07:55.659 --> 00:08:00.110
in Montreal in the early 1920s. Here Victor
gained the trust of a wealthy banker named

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:04.719
Linus Merton: he first had his wallet stolen,
and then returned it intact. He then lured

00:08:04.719 --> 00:08:09.300
him into a gambling scheme: Victor said that
his cousin Emil – perhaps Dapper Dan – was

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:14.490
able to delay by one minute the telegraph
wires relaying the results of horse races.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:19.419
This vital minute would allow Victor and Merton
to always place winning bets. Lustig convinced

00:08:19.419 --> 00:08:24.669
the banker to place a bet for a whopping 30,000
Dollars. No surprise there, when the Lustig

00:08:24.669 --> 00:08:29.590
cousins disappeared from Canada. Again, they
left behind a victim too ashamed, and too

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:35.820
guilty of bet-fixing, to go to the police.
The next big job took place in 1922, in Missouri.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:36.820
Under the name of Robert Duval,
[Editing suggestion: show a photo of actor

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:37.820
Robert Duval on screen, with the caption ‘Not,
not him, just a coincidence’]

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:42.320
he offered to the American Savings Bank 32,000
Dollars in World War I war bonds, the famous

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:47.420
Liberty bonds, in exchange for 10,000 in cash
and some farmland. At the moment of closing

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:51.540
the deal, he switched the envelope containing
the bonds with an empty one. With a simple

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:56.600
sleight of hand trick, he walked out with
the bonds, the farm AND the cash.

00:08:56.600 --> 00:09:00.449
American Savings hired some private detectives
who succeeded in tracking Lustig in Kansas

00:09:00.449 --> 00:09:05.800
City. Victor could rely on his ultimate weapon:
smooth talk. He convinced the detectives that

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:09.910
if the bank pressed charges and the story
went public, then clients would lose confidence

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:13.050
in them. That would spell ruin for American
Savings.

00:09:13.050 --> 00:09:17.450
So, not only did he convince the bank to drop
all the charges – they even paid him an

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:21.740
extra $1000 to buy his silence!
The Eiffel Job

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:26.610
The run-in with the private detectives was
not the only time that Victor Lustig had risked

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:30.550
ending behind bars. In fact, he had been arrested
around 40 times, but every time he managed

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:34.260
to talk or bribe himself out of those sticky
situations. In extreme cases, he simply had

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:36.579
his wife bail him out, for him to disappear
again.

00:09:36.579 --> 00:09:39.630
Victor’s wife was called Roberta Noret,
a pretty, young, red-head Kansan whom he had

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:43.699
married on the 3rd of November 1919. But more
on Victor’s private life later – for now,

00:09:43.699 --> 00:09:48.360
let’s stick to talking shop.
In May 1925 Victor and Dapper Dan moved to

00:09:48.360 --> 00:09:51.620
Paris, possibly to escape increased attention
from American law enforcement. In the Paris

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:53.880
capital they read an article about the status
of disrepair of the Eiffel tower and how maintenance

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:57.410
expenses would be too costly for local government.
That sparked the idea for Victor’s craziest

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:00.620
scam: he would sell the Eiffel Tower.
The idea actually was not that crazy. The

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:04.270
Tower had been erected in 1889 as a temporary
structure, to be taken down no later than

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:07.400
1909, and yet it was still standing. It was
effectively expensive to maintain, and the

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:13.250
public opinion was split about it, many considering
it an eye sore.

00:10:13.250 --> 00:10:16.850
So how about the French Government decided
to sell it as scrap metal?

00:10:16.850 --> 00:12:18.630
Lustig carefully prepared his con. He first
forged a set of stationery for the

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:23.150
Ministry in charge of posts and telegraph.
This was realistic, as the Tower was used

00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:28.170
as a giant post to transmit radio signals.
He then used the letter head to summon the

00:12:28.170 --> 00:12:32.810
five largest scrap metal dealers in Paris.
They were invited to a secret meeting at the

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:34.100
exclusive Hôtel de Crillon, in Place de la
Concorde.

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:37.680
Here was the deal: the French Government was
considering tearing down the Eiffel Tower

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:42.089
and sell its pieces as scrap metal. Lustig
insisted that the five dealers kept quiet

00:12:42.089 --> 00:12:45.720
about it, as the Government did not want the
deal to leak to the press. At the meeting,

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:50.579
Victor identified the fish he was going to
catch: the conveniently named André Poisson.

00:12:50.579 --> 00:12:53.949
His business was relatively small compared
to the others’ and because of that was low

00:12:53.949 --> 00:12:57.470
in self-esteem.
Lustig arranged a separate meeting with Poisson,

00:12:57.470 --> 00:13:02.020
and expressing sympathy for his budding business,
offered the deal to him … in exchange for

00:13:02.020 --> 00:13:06.589
a sizeable kickback. And it was thus that
Count Victor Lustig, not only sold the Eiffel

00:13:06.589 --> 00:13:10.850
Tower as a heap of scrap metal, but he also
pocketed a nice bribe on the side.

00:13:10.850 --> 00:13:14.230
The following day Victor and Dan left France
for Vienna and waited patiently for the storm

00:13:14.230 --> 00:13:18.730
to calm down. To their surprise, though, the
French newspapers did not report the scam.

00:13:18.730 --> 00:13:22.920
Lustig correctly assumed that Poisson had
been too embarrassed to press charges.

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:28.100
It was too good to be true. What did he do
next? Well, he returned to Paris and attempted

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:32.980
the exact same scam a second time – with
different metal dealers of course.

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:36.540
But this second time around one of the dealers
was immediately suspicious and reported this

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:40.860
self-described Ministry official to the police.
Victor was quick to flee: using one of his

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:44.750
many aliases and forged passports he embarked
again and returned to America.

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:54.540
Back to the USA
Upon returning to the US, Lustig set to work

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:58.110
again. He clearly got a kick out of performing
his confidence tricks, but he also needed

00:13:58.110 --> 00:14:02.060
to fund a very expensive lifestyle. He and
his wife Roberta had had a daughter, Bettie

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:06.290
Jean and their living arrangements were very
costly. Roberta and Bettie Jean moved constantly

00:14:06.290 --> 00:14:10.569
from town to town, living in hotels. This
was Lustig’s idea to keep them ‘secret’

00:14:10.569 --> 00:14:14.009
and avoid that they could be traced back to
him to prevent capture.

00:14:14.009 --> 00:14:17.819
To make up for this unconventional lifestyle
Victor lavished them with gifts. When the

00:14:17.819 --> 00:14:21.310
girl reached school age, he signed her up
in an expensive Catholic boarding school in

00:14:21.310 --> 00:14:25.120
Pittsburgh, run by an order of nuns. Because,
you know, you have to give your children a

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:28.029
moral example (!).
On top of this, Lustig had to look after his

00:14:28.029 --> 00:14:33.649
second unofficial wife, or rather long-term
mistress: Billie Mae Scheible, a notorious

00:14:33.649 --> 00:14:36.520
brothel madame.
The long-suffering Roberta was patient enough

00:14:36.520 --> 00:14:40.920
to put up with being basically just a courier
for bail money, but she could not tolerate

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:46.120
his husband’s affair with Billie Mae, nor
the additional philandering on the side. So,

00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:48.810
she divorced him.
They had a heart-warming reunion in which

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:53.699
Roberta literally tried to kill him, by running
him over with her car. But he was quick on

00:14:53.699 --> 00:14:58.040
the dodge … and apparently that was enough
for them to reconcile. Victor and Roberta

00:14:58.040 --> 00:15:03.472
remarried – because Lustig had to do everything
twice, it seems. And divorced again, after

00:15:03.472 --> 00:15:09.160
Roberta found out that Victor was lodging
Billie Mae in the same hotel as hers! Oh,

00:15:09.160 --> 00:15:11.089
the cheek! The scarred left cheek![TA3]
While Lustig was not working his charms with

00:15:11.089 --> 00:15:13.870
the ladies, he was always looking for new
tricks.

00:15:13.870 --> 00:15:18.899
He probably thought that scamming schmucks
was too easy. So he set on scamming Chicago’s

00:15:18.899 --> 00:15:23.949
most dangerous resident: Al Capone[TA4] . The
plan was basic: Lustig convinced Capone to

00:15:23.949 --> 00:15:28.320
loan him 50,000 Dollars to buy some stock
that would guarantee a sure profit. He then

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:32.310
deposited the cash in a bank … and just
waited. After some weeks Victor returned to

00:15:32.310 --> 00:15:37.019
Capone and told him that the stocks had crashed
and the money … it was gone. But, to apologise

00:15:37.019 --> 00:15:40.300
he would return the 50 grand to the gangster,
from his own pocket.

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:43.960
Capone did not realise that Lustig was simply
returning him his own money! And to reward

00:15:43.960 --> 00:15:49.319
his honesty, he gave him 5,000 Dollars.
The Secret Service is on the Case

00:15:49.319 --> 00:15:55.720
Victor’s next money-making scheme was literally
about making money.

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:59.230
In the early 1930s, at the height of the Great
Depression, Victor got into counterfeiting

00:15:59.230 --> 00:16:04.339
with two new accomplices, a chemist named
Tom Shaw and an engraver, William Watts.

00:16:04.339 --> 00:16:08.820
They were so successful that they managed
to produce and circulate over one million

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:12.040
Dollars’ worth of fake money. The Treasury
was so concerned that there was a genuine

00:16:12.040 --> 00:16:16.529
worry this would imbalance the US economy.
This was one step too far, and it raised the

00:16:16.529 --> 00:16:21.440
attention of the Secret Service, one agent
in particular: Italo-American Peter Rubano.

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:24.370
[Roo-bah-know]
Which is ironic, because ‘Rubano’ means

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:27.660
‘They steal’ in Italian.
For our friends who are not familiar with

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:32.040
US law enforcement let me clarify one thing.
Today the Secret Service is associated with

00:16:32.040 --> 00:16:36.450
personal protection of the President and other
high-ranking offices. Its original task however

00:16:36.450 --> 00:16:41.189
was – and still is - to track down and arrest
counterfeiters. It was only after President

00:16:41.189 --> 00:16:45.030
McKinley’s assassination that the Secret
Service was assigned to protect the President,

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:49.649
because at the time they were the only agency
with a Federal jurisdiction.[TA5]

00:16:49.649 --> 00:16:54.759
Fun fact: do you know how Secret Service personnel
introduce themselves? “Hello, I am a Secret

00:16:54.759 --> 00:16:56.649
Agent[TA6] ” – which kind of defeats the
purpose …

00:16:56.649 --> 00:16:57.649
“Hello I am Secret Agent Rubano, nice to
meet you!”

00:16:57.649 --> 00:16:58.649
“My pleasure, I am Undercover Federal Agent
Johnny Utah, how do you do?”

00:16:58.649 --> 00:16:59.649
“I don’t believe we have met, my name
is Blofeld. I am the secretive leader of a

00:16:59.649 --> 00:17:00.649
shadowy criminal network. Oh, and of the Parent-Teachers
association, too, of course!”

00:17:00.649 --> 00:17:01.649
[Subtitle on screen: OK that’s enough, can
we get on with Lustig please?] [TA7]

00:17:01.649 --> 00:17:02.649
Secret Agent Rubano was able to trace back
the counterfeit money to a conman with a number

00:17:02.649 --> 00:17:06.069
of aliases who had been selling some mysterious
money-making boxes … This had happened when

00:17:06.069 --> 00:17:10.300
Lustig had sold a Rumanian box to a Texan
sheriff. When the lawman had confronted him

00:17:10.300 --> 00:17:14.360
about the hoax, Victor had appeased him with
a bribe in fake cash.

00:17:14.360 --> 00:17:18.010
Rubano and the Secret Service established
a connection between the counterfeiters and

00:17:18.010 --> 00:17:22.579
the money box man, but they could not pin
down Lustig. By now, he had taken to traveling

00:17:22.579 --> 00:17:26.800
with a trunk full of disguises, including
rabbi, priest and bellhop.

00:17:26.800 --> 00:17:32.060
Finally, in May 1935, Rubano received an anonymous
tip off from a jealous mistress of Lustig’s.

00:17:32.060 --> 00:17:36.820
Maybe Billie Mae, maybe one of his many other
girlfriends. The Agents converged onto a hotel

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:40.580
in New York.
Count Victor Robert Lustig Miller was handcuffed

00:17:40.580 --> 00:17:44.240
just outside the hotel. The Secret Agents
found a key in his pocket, which turned out

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:49.650
to be for a locker in the Times Square subway
station. Inside, they found a stash of plates

00:17:49.650 --> 00:17:53.750
and chemicals used by his gang to print money.
An admirative agent told Victor that he must

00:17:53.750 --> 00:17:57.900
be the smoothest conman in the world. Victor
shook his head.

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:01.580
“I wouldn’t say that. After all, you have
conned me”.

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:03.610
Final Curtain
Lustig was put behind bars in the Federal

00:18:03.610 --> 00:18:07.131
House of Detention in New York, awaiting trial.
But he had another trick up his sleeve. He

00:18:07.131 --> 00:18:11.580
soon realised that nobody was keeping count
of the bedsheets distributed to inmates. So,

00:18:11.580 --> 00:18:14.740
little by little, he stashed enough bedsheets
to fashion a rope.

00:18:14.740 --> 00:18:18.820
On the 1st of September 1935, he cut through
the wires in the washroom window and swung

00:18:18.820 --> 00:18:23.331
to safety like a Bohemian Tarzan. To distract
onlookers, he just pulled a rag from his pocket

00:18:23.331 --> 00:18:27.010
and pretended to clean the Federal House’s
windows. Then, with a final jump, he landed

00:18:27.010 --> 00:18:31.390
on the pavement, bowed to his audience of
passers-by and disappeared into the night.

00:18:31.390 --> 00:18:35.270
Lustig did not evade the chasers for long,
though. He hid for a period in his daughter’s

00:18:35.270 --> 00:18:41.000
boarding school in Pittsburgh, until on the
28th of September FBI agent G. K. Firestone

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:45.840
and Secret Service agent Fred Gruber spotted
him ducking in a car driven by a chauffeur.

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:48.730
The two ‘feds’ leapt into their car and
gave chase.

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:53.210
The engines roared, neck-and-neck for nine
blocks. The agents yelled to the runaways

00:18:53.210 --> 00:18:57.640
to stop, but Lustig’s driver would keep
on accelerating. The agents had no choice:

00:18:57.640 --> 00:19:01.650
they jerked the steering wheel sideways and
rammed their car into Victor’s. The cars

00:19:01.650 --> 00:19:05.460
crashed to a halt. The agents pulled out their
guns and we like to think that they yelled

00:19:05.460 --> 00:19:10.270
“Stick ‘em up, Lustig!”. What we do
know is that Victor, smooth as silk and cool

00:19:10.270 --> 00:19:11.870
as a cucumber told his captors:
“Well, boys, here I am.”

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:15.090
In November of 1935 Victor Lustig was tried
and sentenced to spend 20 years in prison.

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:19.530
Not just any prison, Alcatraz prison. For
the following years, the man of many aliases

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:24.280
was simply known as “Convict nr 300”.
Amongst other celebrities who shared the Alcatraz

00:19:24.280 --> 00:19:28.800
facilities with him, there was none other
than his former mark, Al Capone, by now a

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:33.130
syphilitic shadow of his former self.
After many years on the prison known as “The

00:19:33.130 --> 00:19:39.050
Rock”, also Victor’s health declined.
By the 7th of December 1946 he had filed 1,192

00:19:39.050 --> 00:19:43.580
medical requests, most of which were ignored,
as the wardens thought they were part of another

00:19:43.580 --> 00:19:47.990
escape plan. Eventually Lustig was transferred
to a secure hospital in Missouri, where doctors

00:19:47.990 --> 00:19:52.810
realized too late that he was not faking.
There, in a town called Springfield, the Conman

00:19:52.810 --> 00:19:57.780
Known as Count Victor Lustig died of pneumonia
on the 11th of March 1947.

00:19:57.780 --> 00:20:01.680
His death certificate stated as occupation:
“Apprentice salesman”.

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:06.260
Many years later, in March of 2015, Czech
historian Tomáš Anděl, from Lustig’s

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:11.550
home town of Hostinné, set out to search
for records and evidence to clarify the mystery

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:15.940
of Victor’s identity. Andel browsed through
historical documents, electoral rolls and

00:20:15.940 --> 00:20:19.890
even school registers. Eventually he had to
admit defeat. There wasn’t, there isn’t

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:26.690
any evidence that Lustig was ever born.
10 steps to becoming a Lustig

00:20:26.690 --> 00:20:32.290
He may have not left us with a clear answer
on his identity, but Victor Lustig did leave

00:20:32.290 --> 00:20:37.510
us with a clear set of rules on how to pull
the perfect con. And here they are

00:20:37.510 --> 00:20:43.640
1. Be a patient listener: it is this, not
fast talking, that gets a con man his coups

00:20:43.640 --> 00:20:47.400
2. Never look bored.
3. Wait for the other person to reveal any

00:20:47.400 --> 00:20:52.150
political opinions, then agree with them.
4. Let the other person reveal religious views,

00:20:52.150 --> 00:20:56.780
then have the same ones.
5. Hint at sex talk, but don’t follow it

00:20:56.780 --> 00:20:59.900
up unless the other fellow shows a strong
interest.

00:20:59.900 --> 00:21:04.100
6. Never discuss illness, unless some special
concern is shown.

00:21:04.100 --> 00:21:09.630
7. Never pry into a person’s personal circumstances:
they’ll tell you all, eventually.

00:21:09.630 --> 00:21:13.900
8. Never boast. Just let your importance be
quietly obvious.

00:21:13.900 --> 00:21:17.880
9. Never be untidy.
10. Never get drunk.

00:21:17.880 --> 00:21:22.950
We at Biographics do not endorse using this
advice to swindle, scam or bamboozle your

00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:27.290
fellow human beings … however we must admit
that this is pretty good advice when it comes

00:21:27.290 --> 00:21:31.290
to engaging with someone and to creating a
meaningful connection. After all, these rules

00:21:31.290 --> 00:21:40.120
could apply to a successful first date – maybe
except for nr 5 – or to a job interview.

00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:44.580
And maybe that’s the key to Victor Lustig’s
illegal but incredible successes. Not the

00:21:44.580 --> 00:21:50.160
elaborate Rumanian boxes, not the sleight
of hand, and not even the counterfeiting skills.

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:54.840
But simply his ability to connect with others,
charm them and making them feel important

00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:56.990
– before robbing them blind.
Conclusion

00:21:56.990 --> 00:21:59.760
Thank you for staying until the end, as it
is my habit I will ask you to like, share

00:21:59.760 --> 00:22:04.850
and subscribe. And if you have a minute why
not leave us a comment. Should we give Victor

00:22:04.850 --> 00:22:12.990
Lustig a movie? And who would you cast in
the lead role? That’s all from me, as usual

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:18.710
– thank you for watching.

